					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mark-penrice" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1858819); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1858819&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973845" alt="Mark Penrice" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1858819)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mark-penrice" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1858819); return false;" >Mark Penrice</a></span> <span class="odate time_1546750076 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Jan 2019 04:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4110084">
									

<p>Took a while to catch on to what was happening, but then it was just a case of holding tight and seeing how far you were willing to take it.</p>
<p>Out of interest, is the messing with the timescale deliberate? The exam was supposed to be over after 8 hours, but there's one test that seems to take 10 hours in of itself, and the rather wacky light wavelength testing (a 2mm modulation starting at 0.01nm would cover all the way from far-UV to mid-IR, wouldn't it?) would itself need more than 8 hours (1 metre range broken up into 2mm steps of a minute each = 500 minutes&#8230;). That latter one bugged me when I first encountered it, especially as if you were going over such a wide range of the EM spectrum you'd use progressively lengthening or shortening steps in proportion with where you were in the band, so you'd retain detail in the shorter wavelengths but not spend ages dragging slowly through the longer ones. Maybe use frequency steps instead, even, though those are also subject to exponential scaling merely in the opposite direction&#8230;</p>

							</div>

								
					
