					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jack-ike" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1618170); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1618170&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973845" alt="Jack Ike" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1618170)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jack-ike" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1618170); return false;" >Jack Ike</a></span> <span class="odate time_1546751498 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Jan 2019 05:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4110119">
									

<p>The light wavelength's timescale was an error on my part. I was originally going to do the exponentially scaling increments, but the maths was far beyond me and having a high resolution seemed irrelevant, considering it's not expected to do anything; if (or rather, when) it does do something in a certain band, they were likely planning to do a future test focusing on that specific band but with a higher degree of revolution, repeating until they got the specific wavelength without using so much time.</p>
<p>As for the remainder of the timescale tampering: yes, it is intentional. The exam was supposed to only go for eight hours, but said timeframe is enforced by the examiner.</p>

